   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT authorizing the town of Southampton, county of Suffolk, to enact by a\nlocal law a homestead exemption and cap on assessment increases for real\nproperty tax purposes",
    "basePrintNo": "S2094",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2094-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Taxation",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2094\n\nSPONSOR: PALUMBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act authorizing the town of Southampton, county of Suffolk, to enact\nby a local law a homestead exemption and cap on assessment increases for\nreal property tax purposes\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nAuthorizes the town of Southampton, county of Suffolk, to enact a home-\nstead exemption and cap on assessment increases for real property tax\npurposes.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Authorizes the town of Southampton, county of Suffolk to\nenact by local law a homestead exemption from real property taxation.\nEligibility for such exemption shall be the same as for the basic school\ntax relief (STAR) exemption pursuant to subdivision 3 of section 425 of\nthe real property tax law. The exemption shall be uniform for all eligi-\nble real property and shall not exceed $50,000 in full assessed value.\n\nSection 2. In addition to the homestead exemption provided for in\nsection one of this act, the town of Southampton also may enact a local\nlaw to cap the amount the value of a property may increase for tax\npurposes, due solely to market trend adjustments at 5% in a given year.\nThe full value would be reset upon property transfer.\n\nSection 3. Effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nProperty taxes are the primary funding source for most local services\nincluding police, fire, ambulance, libraries, roads and schools.  Indi-\nvidual property taxes are determined by calculating the fractional share\nof the total sum to be collected based on a property's value as compared\nto the full value of all taxable real property within the taxing\ndistrict. Each property is assessed a value for the land and a separate\nvalue for any structures built upon the property. The combined total is\nthe \"total assessed value\" of the property. The basic principle is the\nmore a property is worth, the more it will pay in property taxes. The\ntotal needed to be collected across the district is divided by the full\nvalue of all properties to determine the \"tax rate\". The assessed value\nof a property is then multiplied by the tax rate to determine the prop-\nerty's taxes due for that property.\n\nSouthampton is one of the only towns on Long Island that uses a system\nof \"full value\" assessment. The town routinely updates the assessment\nrolls to account for new construction as well as changes to the real\nestate market (market trend adjustment). The unique real estate market\nof the east end has recently and over time seen large spikes in property\nvalues, particularly in areas dominated by seasonal homes. Although the\ngoal of the town of Southampton's system is based on fairness, some\nproperty owners have expressed concerns about the financial stress\ncaused by accelerating tax bills, based solely on market trend adjust-\nments to their assessed valuation. For example, a retired school teacher\nbought a waterfront home in north Sea in the 1960s. She never improved\nthe property with any additions, swimming pools or accessory buildings.\nNonetheless, the value has increased tenfold. Recent years have seen a\nyearly increase of 20% per year in property value. While the teacher is\nthrilled with the tremendous return on her investment, her pension\nincome does not increase each year and her property taxes are now forc-\ning her to consider selling the home where she had hoped to spend her\nretirement.\n\nIn 2019, the Southampton Town Board enacted a two-year freeze on market\ntrend adjustments to property assessments in an effort to address commu-\nnity concerns about the effects of the town of Southampton's assessment\nsystem on individuals like the teacher described in the above example.\nThe town board individuals like the teacher described in the above exam-\nple. The town board assembled a technical advisory committee to review\nour system and make recommendations. The committee found that, in gener-\nal the town of Southampton's system was better than other town's that\nonly assessed upon new construction. In fact, because of the town of\nSouthampton's system, more affluent areas of the town have been picking\nup a larger and larger percentage share of the costs of funding town\noperations because these areas have accelerated in value at a faster\nrate than other areas of the town of Southampton. This has helped keep\nproperty taxes low in working class communities.\n\nRather than abandon the town of Southampton's system, the advisory\ncommittee recommended changes to address the particular type of problem\nillustrated above. This legislation is necessary to implement two\nspecific fixes by I) enacting a \"Homestead Exemption\" reducing the\nassessed value for taxation purposes of all year round home by $50,000\nand 2) cap the amount the value of a property may increase for tax\npurposes, due solely to market trend adjustments at 5% in a given year.\nThe full value would be reset upon property transfer.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022 S.5984/A.6667.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
